Transaction A reads a specific row. Concurrently, Transaction B updates that same row and commits its changes. When Transaction A attempts to read the same row again, it retrieves the new, committed value, leading to an inconsistent view within Transaction A.
```sql
-- Transaction A
START TRANSACTION;
SELECT Balance FROM Accounts WHERE AccountID = 1; -- First read
-- Transaction B (concurrently)
START TRANSACTION;
UPDATE Accounts SET Balance = Balance + 50 WHERE AccountID = 1;
COMMIT;
-- Transaction A
SELECT Balance FROM Accounts WHERE AccountID = 1; -- Second read, different value
COMMIT;
```

Transaction A queries the count of products in a specific category. Concurrently, Transaction B inserts a new product into that same category and commits. When Transaction A executes the same count query again, it finds a different (higher) number of rows.
```sql
-- Transaction A
START TRANSACTION;
SELECT COUNT(*) FROM Products WHERE Category = 'Electronics'; -- First read
-- Transaction B (concurrently)
START TRANSACTION;
INSERT INTO Products (Name, Category) VALUES ('New Gadget', 'Electronics');
COMMIT;
-- Transaction A
SELECT COUNT(*) FROM Products WHERE Category = 'Electronics'; -- Second read, different count
COMMIT;
```
